  migrated side, on the same principle: an empty coverage data set that yields a pass is a failure
  regardless of a zero exit code, and an artifact that looks plausible while measuring nothing real is
  worse than an honest smaller one.

  The rule against disabling failing tests is why this file exists at all. It makes the claim that
  nothing was disabled in this class verifiable rather than merely asserted. The skipped count is 0 and
  no skipped element appears anywhere. The class carries no ignore annotation, and any appearance of
  one would be an increase and a violation, whether in the source or in the reactor's unit test plugin
  configuration. No test exclusion entry names this class; the reactor's declaration of that plugin
  carries no exclusion entry at all, and an exclusion would in any case require a row of the baseline
  test failures register, which a method whose name merely mentions an exception or invalid input can
  never supply. Across the fourteen archived unit test classes of this module the count of ignore
  annotations is zero, and that statement is scoped to those fourteen deliberately, because one
  integration test class of this module does carry one and is executed by the integration test plugin
